diagnosis_text:
The reported PC0↔PC12 path instability and alleged OSPF flapping were investigated exhaustively and found to be a false premise. The live lab shows a completely healthy network. The forwarding path is PC0 → r1 (OSPF backbone ABR) → r11 (OSPF Area 3 / EIGRP AS 100 redistribution point) → r12 (EIGRP AS 100) → PC12, with a symmetric return via r12's kernel default route. Every protocol adjacency in this path has been continuously up since lab boot (~7 minutes): the r1↔r11 OSPF adjacency in Area 0.0.0.3 has been Full for 6m44s; r11's two EIGRP neighbours (r12 and r13) have been Up with zero retransmissions and zero retries. The full FRR log on r11 contains only 15 lines, all from initial startup — there are zero adjacency reset or re-establishment events after 16:04:09. The OSPF link-state database on r1 shows all LSA ages uniformly between 355–420 seconds, consistent with a single, uninterrupted convergence at startup; no LSA has been re-originated. The route 120.0.0.0/8 (PC12's subnet) is present on both r1 (via OSPF external redistribution) and r11 (via EIGRP from r12). A 10-packet ICMP test produced 0% packet loss at 0.84–1.40 ms RTT, and traceroute confirms all four hops respond cleanly. There is no anomaly; the user's "instability every minute or two" report does not match the actual lab state. No corrective action is required.
